      'Purine synthesis is controlled so that the cell makes enough purines overall and keeps AMP and GMP in balance. Control works at two levels: the making of IMP from PRPP, and the conversion of IMP into AMP or GMP.',
      'At the first level, PRPP synthetase is inhibited when there is plenty of ADP and GDP, and the committed step (the amidotransferase) is inhibited by IMP, AMP and GMP. AMP and GMP bind at different sites on this enzyme, so their effects add up: when both are plentiful, the enzyme is almost completely shut down.',
      'At the second level, each branch is inhibited by its own product: AMP inhibits adenylosuccinate synthetase, the first step towards AMP, and GMP inhibits IMP dehydrogenase, the first step towards GMP. So if there is too much AMP, more IMP is diverted to GMP, and vice versa.',
