      'In animals, pyrimidine synthesis is controlled mainly at its first step, carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II. The end products UDP and UTP inhibit the enzyme, so when there are plenty of pyrimidines, synthesis slows. ATP and PRPP activate it: ATP signals that energy is available, and PRPP signals that the sugar-phosphate needed later in the pathway is available. Glutamine analogues such as azaserine block it. UMP and CMP also inhibit OMP decarboxylase.',
      'In bacteria such as E. coli, the main control point is the second step, aspartate transcarbamoylase (ATCase), a classic allosteric enzyme. CTP, the end product of the pathway, inhibits it by reducing its affinity for its substrates, without changing its maximum rate. ATP activates it, competing with CTP for the regulatory sites. The logic is that ATP is a purine: when purines and energy are plentiful, the cell should make pyrimidines too, so that it has a balanced supply for copying DNA, and when CTP is abundant, it should stop.',
