      'Both purine and pyrimidine synthesis produce ribonucleotides, with ribose as the sugar. DNA, however, needs deoxyribonucleotides. These are made from ribonucleotides by ribonucleotide reductase, which removes the oxygen from carbon 2′ of the ribose in nucleoside diphosphates, replacing it with hydrogen. The electrons for this reduction come ultimately from NADPH, passed through the small proteins thioredoxin or glutaredoxin.',
      'Thymine nucleotides are a special case, because thymine is found only in DNA. They are made from uracil nucleotides. dUTP is first broken down to dUMP by dUTPase, which also has the useful effect of preventing uracil from being built into DNA by mistake. Then thymidylate synthase adds a methyl group to dUMP, making dTMP. The methyl group comes from N⁵,N¹⁰-methylene-tetrahydrofolate. Unusually, in this reaction the folate is oxidised to dihydrofolate. To be reused, it must be reduced back to tetrahydrofolate by dihydrofolate reductase, using NADPH, and then reloaded with a carbon unit from serine.',
      'Because dividing cells need large amounts of dTMP, this pathway is a major target for cancer drugs. Fluorouracil, converted in the body to fluorodeoxyuridylate, binds irreversibly to thymidylate synthase and inactivates it. Methotrexate and aminopterin block dihydrofolate reductase, so the cell runs out of tetrahydrofolate. Trimethoprim blocks the bacterial version of this enzyme. These drugs also affect normal, rapidly dividing cells, explaining their side effects: hair loss, damage to the bone marrow and immune system and ulcers in the gut.',
