      'Pyrimidines are broken down differently from purines. The nucleotides first lose their phosphate and sugar, and cytosine is converted to uracil by removing its amino group. In the liver, the ring of uracil and thymine is then broken open, starting with a reduction rather than an oxidation.',
      'The end products are small and soluble: uracil (and so cytosine) gives β-alanine, and thymine gives β-aminoisobutyrate. These can be converted into malonyl-CoA and methylmalonyl-CoA, which enter central metabolism, together with ammonia and carbon dioxide. Since these products are soluble, defects in pyrimidine breakdown do not cause crystal diseases such as gout.',
